A baseball pitcher can throw a ball 300 feet away from home plate. A baseball pitcher throws a fastball at 90 mph. How long does it take the ball to reach home plate?
Answer:
The distance is equal to product of speed and time.

$$
\begin{gather*}
300 = 90 \cdot t
\end{gather*}
$$

Divide both sides by 90

$$
\begin{gather*}
\frac{300}{90} = \frac{90 \cdot t}{90}
\end{gather*}
$$

We can't divide both side by 90 anymore. If we need to divide the equation we will divide the numerator by the denominator. So we have to write the equation in fraction form

$$
\begin{gather*}
\frac{300}{90} = \frac{90t}{90}
\end{gather*}
$$
The numerator is on the right side of equation and the denominator is on the left side of the equation. The denominator in left side is the denominator of the original equation. Multiply both sides with the denominator in left side

$$
\begin{gather*}
300 = 90t
\end{gather*}
$$
Divide the numerator with denominator in second step. We have $300 : 90 = 3$

$$
\begin{gather*}
300 : 90 = 3
\end{gather*}
$$

