Convert the given improper fraction to a mixed number so that multiplying the improper fraction by a mixed number become easier to evaluate. To convert an improper fraction to a mixed number divide the numerator by the denominator of the fraction. The quotient is the whole number, the remainder is the new numerator of the mixed number and the denominator of the fraction remains the same, therefore:

$$
\begin{equation*}
\dfrac{1}{2}\times\dfrac{2}{3}=\dfrac{1\times2}{2\times3}
\end{equation*}
$$

Evaluate the product of the numerator and denominator of fraction 1:

$$
\begin{equation*}
=\dfrac{2}{2\times3}
\end{equation*}
$$

Evaluate the product of the denominator of the fraction 2:

$$
\begin{equation*}
=\dfrac{2}{2\times3}= \dfrac{2}{6}
\end{equation*}
$$

Divide the numerator of the fraction to obtain the new numerator:

$$
\begin{equation*}
= \dfrac{1\times2}{2\times3}=\dfrac{2}{6}= \dfrac{1}{3}
\end{equation*}
$$
$$
\dfrac{1}{2}\times\dfrac{2}{3}=\dfrac{1}{3}
$$

